rangeOfComposedCharacterSequencesForRange must use the LAST CHARACTER IN the range.

It asked for the cluster at NSMaxRange(range), which is ONE PAST THE END and, for the common case
of a range covering the whole string, is the length itself. The cluster lookup answers nothing
there, so the computed length collapsed and the method returned an EMPTY range for a range that
covers real text. Out of bounds arguments were not checked either, and an empty range fell into the
same arithmetic.

WHAT IT COST, measured rather than reasoned. iA Writer builds every library row excerpt through
this. content_excerpt(), a custom SQLite function inside Kit.framework at Kit+0x624a7, is:

    if (sqlite3_value_type(argv[0]) == SQLITE_NULL) return sqlite3_result_null(ctx);
    NSData *d = [[NSData alloc] initWithBytesNoCopy:sqlite3_value_text(argv[0]) ...];
    IALibraryIndexContent *c = [[IALibraryIndexContent alloc] initWithColumnData:d];
    NSRange r = c.contentRange;  r.length = MIN(r.length, 360);
    r = [c.text safeRangeForRange:r];
    r = [c.text rangeOfComposedCharacterSequencesForRange:r];      <- HERE
    sqlite3_result_text64(ctx, [[c.text substringWithRange:r] UTF8String], ...);

An empty range makes an empty excerpt, the excerpt column comes back null, IALibraryItem has no
excerptProvider, and the row draws "Updating..." because that is what it draws when the excerpt
string is empty. Every library row said Updating... for months and this was the reason.

BEFORE and AFTER, from the same drive with cider-spy watching the two accessors:

  before  IALibraryIndexItemResultSet.excerpt -> nil x20,  IALibraryItem.excerptProvider -> nil x56
  after   excerpt -> "# Cider", "# Probe", "# Second", "test", and excerptProvider returns real
          IALibraryValueProvider objects. The rows draw their text.

The remaining nils are correct: a folder row and a genuinely empty document.
